1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,043
Aarav finds two rules stuck
on the fridge at home.

2
00:00:03,343 --> 00:00:04,243
Rule one.

3
00:00:04,121 --> 00:00:07,384
No games, unless homework
and chores are both done.

4
00:00:07,684 --> 00:00:08,584
Rule two.

5
00:00:08,452 --> 00:00:12,122
No games if homework is not
done, or chores are not done.

6
00:00:12,422 --> 00:00:13,529
Different words.

7
00:00:13,529 --> 00:00:14,429
Same rule?

8
00:00:14,351 --> 00:00:16,733
Boolean algebra can tell us for sure.

9
00:00:17,233 --> 00:00:21,758
Boolean algebra is maths with
only two values, one and zero.

10
00:00:22,108 --> 00:00:23,858
One means true, or on.

11
00:00:23,858 --> 00:00:25,759
Zero means false, or off.

12
00:00:26,109 --> 00:00:28,016
It is named after George Boole.

13
00:00:28,016 --> 00:00:30,515
He wrote its rules in eighteen fifty four.

14
00:00:30,865 --> 00:00:32,504
It has just three moves.

15
00:00:32,504 --> 00:00:33,806
AND, OR and NOT.

16
00:00:34,156 --> 00:00:35,893
AND is written as a dot.

17
00:00:35,893 --> 00:00:37,631
OR is written as a plus.

18
00:00:37,981 --> 00:00:40,204
NOT is written as a bar on top.

19
00:00:40,204 --> 00:00:41,618
A bar means not A.

20
00:00:42,268 --> 00:00:44,271
The plus here is not adding.

21
00:00:44,271 --> 00:00:46,125
One plus one is just one.

22
00:00:46,775 --> 00:00:48,175
Now the main laws.

23
00:00:48,175 --> 00:00:50,141
Picture switches and a lamp.

24
00:00:50,491 --> 00:00:51,937
A plus zero is A.

25
00:00:51,937 --> 00:00:54,694
A gap beside the switch changes nothing.

26
00:00:55,344 --> 00:00:56,734
A plus one is one.

27
00:00:56,734 --> 00:00:59,111
A wire beside it keeps the lamp on.

28
00:00:59,761 --> 00:01:01,186
A dot zero is zero.

29
00:01:01,186 --> 00:01:03,979
A gap in line, and the lamp never lights.

30
00:01:04,629 --> 00:01:05,891
A dot one is A.

31
00:01:05,891 --> 00:01:07,988
A wire in line changes nothing.

32
00:01:08,638 --> 00:01:09,866
A plus A is A.

33
00:01:09,866 --> 00:01:11,605
And A dot A is also A.

34
00:01:12,255 --> 00:01:14,271
Now the second switch is A bar.

35
00:01:14,271 --> 00:01:16,287
It is always the opposite of A.

36
00:01:16,637 --> 00:01:19,231
Side by side, one is always closed.

37
00:01:19,231 --> 00:01:21,104
So A plus A bar is one.

38
00:01:21,754 --> 00:01:23,953
In a line, one is always open.

39
00:01:23,953 --> 00:01:25,717
So A dot A bar is zero.

40
00:01:26,367 --> 00:01:28,292
And A with two bars is just A.

41
00:01:28,292 --> 00:01:30,156
Flip twice, and you are back.

42
00:01:30,806 --> 00:01:32,530
Now the stars of this video.

43
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:33,757
De Morgan's theorems.

44
00:01:34,107 --> 00:01:38,433
They are named after Augustus De
Morgan, a British mathematician.

45
00:01:38,783 --> 00:01:39,784
Theorem one.

46
00:01:39,784 --> 00:01:43,787
A plus B, under one long
bar, equals A bar dot B bar.

47
00:01:44,437 --> 00:01:47,078
Break the long bar into two short bars.

48
00:01:47,078 --> 00:01:48,802
Then change plus to dot.

49
00:01:49,152 --> 00:01:50,186
Theorem two.

50
00:01:50,186 --> 00:01:54,321
A dot B, under one long bar,
equals A bar plus B bar.

51
00:01:54,971 --> 00:01:57,764
Again, break the bar,
and change dot to plus.

52
00:01:58,114 --> 00:01:59,883
An easy way to remember it.

53
00:01:59,883 --> 00:02:01,969
Break the line, change the sign.

54
00:02:02,619 --> 00:02:05,861
In gates, a NOR gate equals an
AND gate with bubbled inputs.

55
00:02:06,511 --> 00:02:09,408
And a NAND gate equals an
OR gate with bubbled inputs.

56
00:02:10,058 --> 00:02:12,933
Let's prove theorem two on the breadboard.

57
00:02:12,933 --> 00:02:14,508
We test every input.

58
00:02:14,858 --> 00:02:16,357
On top, a NAND gate.

59
00:02:16,357 --> 00:02:18,793
Below, two NOT gates feed an OR gate.

60
00:02:19,143 --> 00:02:20,202
Both inputs zero.

61
00:02:20,202 --> 00:02:21,352
The top gives one.

62
00:02:21,352 --> 00:02:22,627
The bottom gives one.

63
00:02:22,977 --> 00:02:23,939
Zero and one.

64
00:02:23,939 --> 00:02:24,945
Both give one.

65
00:02:24,945 --> 00:02:25,907
One and zero.

66
00:02:25,907 --> 00:02:26,913
Both give one.

67
00:02:27,263 --> 00:02:28,380
Both inputs one.

68
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:29,687
The top gives zero.

69
00:02:29,687 --> 00:02:31,367
The bottom gives zero too.

70
00:02:31,717 --> 00:02:33,174
All four rows match.

71
00:02:33,174 --> 00:02:35,490
So the two sides are always equal.

72
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:37,299
Watch the scope.

73
00:02:37,299 --> 00:02:40,011
The two output traces
are exactly the same.

74
00:02:40,661 --> 00:02:44,438
The same test proves theorem
one, with a NOR gate on top.

75
00:02:45,088 --> 00:02:46,730
Now back to the fridge.

76
00:02:46,730 --> 00:02:48,266
H means homework done.

77
00:02:48,266 --> 00:02:49,705
C means chores done.

78
00:02:50,055 --> 00:02:53,573
Rule one is H dot C,
with one bar over both.

79
00:02:53,923 --> 00:02:56,394
Rule two is H bar plus C bar.

80
00:02:56,744 --> 00:02:58,877
By theorem two, these are equal.

81
00:02:58,877 --> 00:03:00,871
Both rules mean the same thing.

82
00:03:01,521 --> 00:03:02,490
Break the line.

83
00:03:02,490 --> 00:03:03,501
Change the sign.

84
00:03:04,301 --> 00:03:05,456
Worked example.

85
00:03:05,456 --> 00:03:06,560
Simplify this.

86
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:08,726
One long bar covers everything.

87
00:03:09,076 --> 00:03:09,976
Step one.

88
00:03:09,807 --> 00:03:12,895
Break the long bar, and
change the plus to a dot.

89
00:03:13,595 --> 00:03:14,495
Step two.

90
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:16,325
Each part now has two bars.

91
00:03:16,325 --> 00:03:17,532
Two bars cancel.

92
00:03:18,232 --> 00:03:19,132
Step three.

93
00:03:19,127 --> 00:03:20,118
Multiply out.

94
00:03:20,118 --> 00:03:21,285
A dot A is A.

95
00:03:21,285 --> 00:03:22,957
So we get A plus AB.

96
00:03:23,657 --> 00:03:24,557
Step four.

97
00:03:24,411 --> 00:03:25,311
Take A out.

98
00:03:25,261 --> 00:03:27,100
We get A, times one plus B.

99
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:29,602
One plus anything is one.

100
00:03:29,602 --> 00:03:30,904
So Y is just A.

101
00:03:31,604 --> 00:03:33,493
Check with A one, B zero.

102
00:03:33,493 --> 00:03:35,025
The circuit gives one.

103
00:03:35,025 --> 00:03:36,123
And A is one.

104
00:03:36,773 --> 00:03:40,406
So these three gates do the
same job as a plain wire from A.

105
00:03:41,056 --> 00:03:42,520
Now, the exam trap.

106
00:03:42,870 --> 00:03:46,300
Many students break the bar,
but forget to change the sign.

107
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:50,491
They write A plus B barred,
equals A bar plus B bar.

108
00:03:50,841 --> 00:03:52,852
Test it with A one, B zero.

109
00:03:52,852 --> 00:03:54,511
The true answer is zero.

110
00:03:54,861 --> 00:03:56,563
The wrong form gives one.

111
00:03:56,563 --> 00:03:58,945
Change the sign, and it gives zero.

112
00:03:59,595 --> 00:04:01,278
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:01,278 --> 00:04:05,547
In PUC board papers, and
in ISC Computer Science.

114
00:04:05,897 --> 00:04:10,315
JEE Main has also used De Morgan's
theorem to simplify a gate circuit.

115
00:04:10,665 --> 00:04:14,578
Expect questions to state and prove
both theorems, and simplify circuits.

116
00:04:15,228 --> 00:04:16,128
Try this.

117
00:04:16,077 --> 00:04:19,607
Y is A bar B, barred,
times AB bar, barred.

118
00:04:19,607 --> 00:04:21,253
Which option equals Y?

119
00:04:26,653 --> 00:04:27,553
B.

120
00:04:27,023 --> 00:04:28,711
AB, plus A bar B bar.

121
00:04:29,061 --> 00:04:32,075
Break each bar, and
change each dot to a plus.

122
00:04:32,425 --> 00:04:35,658
We get A plus B bar, times A bar plus B.

123
00:04:36,008 --> 00:04:37,054
Multiply out.

124
00:04:37,054 --> 00:04:38,705
A dot A bar is zero.

125
00:04:38,705 --> 00:04:40,356
B dot B bar is zero.

126
00:04:40,706 --> 00:04:43,770
What is left is AB, plus A bar B bar.

127
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:45,966
Screenshot this card.

128
00:04:49,166 --> 00:04:51,834
Next, XOR and XNOR gates.

129
00:04:51,834 --> 00:04:54,077
That answer has its own gate.
